Cactus Code Thorn TestPersistentGroups
Author(s)    : Liwei Ji
Maintainer(s): Liwei Ji
Licence      : LGPL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

Test that refined-level coarse-fine ghosts of checkpointed grid functions
that the time integrator does not own are refreshed by SYNC under
subcycling, for a default-tagged group (evolved by default) and for a
checkpoint="yes" evolve="no" group. Each group holds a time stamp written on
the interior every step; a check counts the points that hold neither the
level's own stamp nor, on coarse-fine ghosts, the parent level's stamp.

The tests run on top of TestSubcyclingMC2's RK4 evolution with a moving
refined box (regridded every 4 iterations): moving.par uninterrupted, and
checkpoint-moving.par / recover-moving.par as a checkpoint/recover pair with
a mid-cycle checkpoint between two regrids. After recovery the tested groups
are SYNCed and checked before evolution continues.

The opt-in test_history parameter adds a distinct, checkpointed two-timelevel
diagnostic with evolve="no" and restrict="no". It is updated only every other
coarse step. Before each update, both timelevels must contain the previous
value: this tests current-timelevel seeding. After an update, the old timelevel
must remain unchanged. Both timelevels are checked again after moving regrids
and immediately after checkpoint recovery. Recovery checks the stored interiors
before restoring the ghosts omitted by openPMD; no recovered interior is
rewritten. The field is constant in space, and updates occur at common coarse
step boundaries, so the expected history is unambiguous at every regrid.

Run these checks with scripts/test-group-lifecycle.py EXECUTABLE --nprocs 1
(or --nprocs 2). The runner substitutes temporary output/checkpoint directories
in par/history-moving.par, par/history-checkpoint.par and
par/history-recover.par, then runs an uninterrupted evolution and a fresh
checkpoint/recover pair. No checked-in checkpoint files are needed. Every
mismatch is fatal. history_checks records successful cycling (1), an actual
update (2), an evolution-time regrid (4), and recovery (8); termination requires
the relevant bits, so a skipped test phase cannot silently pass. The original
single-timelevel controls and reference outputs remain unchanged when
test_history is disabled.
